      "headline": "Establishment of Camp Dunlap",
      "text": "Camp Dunlap was established during World War II as a training facility for the United States Marine Corps. The camp was constructed in the Sonoran Desert in Imperial County, California.",
      "date": "1942"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decommissioning of Camp Dunlap",
      "text": "After World War II, Camp Dunlap was decommissioned and the buildings were dismantled, leaving behind the concrete slabs that would later give Slab City its name.",
      "date": "1946"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Emergence of Slab City",
      "text": "In the years following the decommissioning of Camp Dunlap, the site began to attract squatters and people seeking an alternative lifestyle, eventually becoming known as Slab City.",
      "date": "1950s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Salvation Mountain",
      "text": "In the 1980s, Leonard Knight began constructing Salvation Mountain, a colorful art installation and religious monument made from adobe, straw, and thousands of gallons of paint, located near Slab City.",
      "date": "1980s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Recognition of Salvation Mountain",
      "text": "Salvation Mountain gained recognition as a significant folk art site, attracting visitors and media attention to Slab City.",
      "date": "2000s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Leonard Knight's Passing",
      "text": "Leonard Knight, the creator of Salvation Mountain, passed away on February 10, 2014, leaving behind his iconic art installation as a legacy.",
      "date": "2014-02-10"
    }
  ]
}

