      "headline": "Commissioning of the Steele Dossier",
      "text": "In June 2016, the Steele dossier was commissioned by Fusion GPS, a private research firm, initially funded by The Washington Free Beacon, a conservative website, and later by the Democratic National Committee and the Hillary Clinton campaign.",
      "date": "2016-06"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Compilation of the Steele Dossier",
      "text": "Between June and December 2016, Christopher Steele, a former British intelligence officer, compiled a series of memos that would become known as the Steele dossier. The memos contained allegations of misconduct and conspiracy between the Trump campaign and the Russian government.",
      "date": "2016-06"
    },
    {
      "headline": "FBI Receives Steele Dossier",
      "text": "In July 2016, Steele provided the FBI with a copy of his findings, expressing concerns about the potential implications of the information he had gathered.",
      "date": "2016-07"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Completion of the Steele Dossier",
      "text": "By December 2016, Steele had completed the dossier, which consisted of 17 memos totaling 35 pages.",
      "date": "2016-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of the Steele Dossier",
      "text": "On January 10, 2017, BuzzFeed News published the Steele dossier in full, despite its unverified status, sparking widespread media coverage and controversy.",
      "date": "2017-01-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Senate Judiciary Committee Hearing",
      "text": "In June 2017, the Senate Judiciary Committee held a hearing to investigate the origins and implications of the Steele dossier.",
      "date": "2017-06"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Inspector General Report",
      "text": "On December 9, 2019, the U.S. Department of Justice's Inspector General released a report on the FBI's Crossfire Hurricane investigation, which included an examination of the Steele dossier's role in the investigation.",
      "date": "2019-12-09"
    }
  ]
}

