      "headline": "Discovery by Europeans",
      "text": "Midway Atoll was discovered by Europeans on July 5, 1859, when Captain N.C. Brooks of the Hawaiian bark Gambia sighted the islands and claimed them for the United States.",
      "date": "1859-07-05"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Annexation by the United States",
      "text": "On August 28, 1867, Midway Atoll was formally annexed by the United States, becoming the first Pacific island annexed by the U.S. government.",
      "date": "1867-08-28"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of a Cable Station",
      "text": "In 1903, the Commercial Pacific Cable Company established a cable station on Midway Atoll as part of the first trans-Pacific telegraph cable.",
      "date": "1903"
    },
    {
      "headline": "World War II - Battle of Midway",
      "text": "From June 4 to June 7, 1942, the Battle of Midway took place, a pivotal naval battle in the Pacific Theater of World War II, where the United States Navy decisively defeated an attacking fleet of the Imperial Japanese Navy.",
      "date": "1942-06-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Designation as a National Wildlife Refuge",
      "text": "In 1988, Midway Atoll was designated as a National Wildlife Refuge, highlighting its importance as a habitat for seabirds and marine life.",
      "date": "1988"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of the Papahānaumokuākea Marine National Monument",
      "text": "On June 15, 2006, President George W. Bush established the Papahānaumokuākea Marine National Monument, which includes Midway Atoll, to protect its unique ecosystem.",
      "date": "2006-06-15"
    }
  ]
}

