      "headline": "Amedeo Avogadro's Birth",
      "text": "Amedeo Avogadro, the Italian physicist and chemist after whom the Avogadro constant is named, was born on August 9, 1776.",
      "date": "1776-08-09"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Avogadro's Hypothesis",
      "text": "In 1811, Amedeo Avogadro proposed that equal volumes of gases, at the same temperature and pressure, contain an equal number of molecules. This hypothesis laid the groundwork for the concept of the Avogadro constant.",
      "date": "1811"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Avogadro's Death",
      "text": "Amedeo Avogadro passed away on July 9, 1856.",
      "date": "1856-07-09"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Estimation of Avogadro's Number",
      "text": "In 1865, Johann Josef Loschmidt made the first estimation of the number of molecules in a given volume of gas, which is related to Avogadro's number.",
      "date": "1865"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Mole Concept",
      "text": "In 1909, Wilhelm Ostwald introduced the concept of the mole, which is directly related to Avogadro's number, as it represents the number of entities in a mole of substance.",
      "date": "1909"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Avogadro Constant Defined",
      "text": "In 1971, the Avogadro constant was officially defined as the number of atoms in 12 grams of carbon-12, which is approximately 6.022 x 10^23 mol^-1.",
      "date": "1971"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Redefinition of the Avogadro Constant",
      "text": "On May 20, 2019, the Avogadro constant was redefined to have an exact value of 6.02214076×10^23 mol^-1 as part of the redefinition of the SI base units.",
      "date": "2019-05-20"
    }
  ]
}

