    {
      "headline": "Origin of the Term 'Potemkin Village'",
      "text": "The term 'Potemkin village' originates from stories about Grigory Potemkin, a Russian field marshal and former lover of Empress Catherine II. In 1787, during Catherine's journey to Crimea, Potemkin allegedly constructed fake portable villages along the Dnieper River to impress the Empress and her foreign guests. These structures were said to be disassembled and reassembled further along her route.",
      "date": "1787"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modern Historians' View",
      "text": "Modern historians generally agree that the accounts of Potemkin's portable villages are exaggerated. While Potemkin did organize an impressive tour for Catherine II, the extent of the deception described in the original stories is likely overstated.",
      "date": "1787"
    }
  ]
}

