{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Origin of Graph Theory",
      "text": "The origin of graph theory can be traced back to 1736 with the publication of Euler's paper on the Seven Bridges of Königsberg. Euler's work laid the foundation for graph theory by solving the problem of whether it was possible to walk through the city of Königsberg and cross each of its seven bridges once and only once.",
      "date": "1736"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Trees",
      "text": "In 1857, Arthur Cayley introduced the concept of trees in graph theory, which are connected graphs with no cycles. Trees became an important structure in graph theory and have numerous applications in computer science.",
      "date": "1857"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Planar Graphs",
      "text": "In 1930, Kazimierz Kuratowski provided a characterization of planar graphs, which can be drawn on a plane without any edges crossing. This was a significant development in graph theory.",
      "date": "1930"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Graph Coloring",
      "text": "Graph coloring, a method of assigning labels to graph elements, was introduced in the 1850s. The four color theorem, which states that any planar graph can be colored with four colors without adjacent vertices sharing the same color, was proven in 1976.",
      "date": "1976"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Emergence of Network Theory",
      "text": "In the mid-20th century, graph theory expanded into network theory, which studies the structure of networks and their dynamics. This has applications in various fields, including sociology, biology, and computer science.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Random Graphs",
      "text": "In 1959, Paul Erdős and Alfréd Rényi introduced the concept of random graphs, which are graphs generated by some random process. This concept has become a fundamental part of probabilistic graph theory.",
      "date": "1959"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Graph Algorithms",
      "text": "In the 1960s and 1970s, significant progress was made in the development of graph algorithms, including algorithms for shortest paths, network flows, and graph isomorphism.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Small-World Networks",
      "text": "In 1998, Duncan Watts and Steven Strogatz introduced the concept of small-world networks, which are characterized by short path lengths and high clustering. This concept has been influential in understanding real-world networks.",
      "date": "1998"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Scale-Free Networks",
      "text": "In 1999, Albert-László Barabási and Réka Albert introduced the concept of scale-free networks, which are networks with a power-law degree distribution. This concept has been important in understanding the structure of the Internet and other complex networks.",
      "date": "1999"
    }
  ]
}

{
