      "headline": "Peak Popularity of Mithraism",
      "text": "Mithraism reached its peak popularity during the 2nd and 3rd centuries AD, particularly among soldiers and merchants across the Roman Empire. The religion was practiced in secretive underground temples known as Mithraea.",
      "date": "0200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decline of Mithraism",
      "text": "By the 4th century AD, Mithraism began to decline as Christianity gained prominence and became the official religion of the Roman Empire. The Edict of Milan in 313 AD, which granted religious tolerance to Christians, marked a significant turning point.",
      "date": "0313"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Suppression of Mithraism",
      "text": "In the late 4th century AD, Mithraism faced suppression as Christian emperors enacted policies against pagan religions. The Theodosian decrees of 391-392 AD effectively banned pagan practices, leading to the closure of Mithraic temples.",
      "date": "0391"
    }
  ]
}

