      "headline": "Becomes Prime Minister of Great Britain",
      "text": "On December 19, 1783, William Pitt the Younger became the Prime Minister of Great Britain, at the age of 24, making him the youngest Prime Minister in British history.",
      "date": "1783-12-19"
    },
    {
      "headline": "India Act 1784",
      "text": "In 1784, Pitt passed the India Act, which reformed the administration of the British East India Company and established a system of dual control by the British government.",
      "date": "1784"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Regency Crisis",
      "text": "During the Regency Crisis of 1788-1789, Pitt opposed the regency of the Prince of Wales during King George III's illness, advocating for minimal powers for the regent.",
      "date": "1788"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Acts of Union 1800",
      "text": "Pitt was instrumental in the passing of the Acts of Union 1800, which united the Kingdom of Great Britain and the Kingdom of Ireland into the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland, effective from January 1, 1801.",
      "date": "1800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Resignation as Prime Minister",
      "text": "Pitt resigned as Prime Minister on March 14, 1801, due to his inability to secure Catholic emancipation following the Acts of Union.",
      "date": "1801-03-14"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Returns as Prime Minister",
      "text": "William Pitt the Younger returned to office as Prime Minister on May 10, 1804, amidst the ongoing Napoleonic Wars.",
      "date": "1804-05-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of William Pitt the Younger",
      "text": "William Pitt the Younger died on January 23, 1806, at the age of 46, in Putney, London, England.",
