      "headline": "Birth of Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec",
      "text": "Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec was born on November 24, 1864, in Albi, France.",
      "date": "1864-11-24"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Accident and Bone Fractures",
      "text": "In 1878, Toulouse-Lautrec broke his right leg, and in 1879, he broke his left leg. These accidents, combined with a genetic disorder, led to his legs ceasing to grow, leaving him with an adult torso and child-sized legs.",
      "date": "1879"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Move to Paris",
      "text": "In 1882, Toulouse-Lautrec moved to Paris to study art under the academic painters Léon Bonnat and Fernand Cormon.",
      "date": "1882"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Exhibition",
      "text": "In 1889, Toulouse-Lautrec held his first exhibition at the Café du Tambourin in Paris.",
      "date": "1889"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Creation of Moulin Rouge Posters",
      "text": "In 1891, Toulouse-Lautrec created a series of posters for the Moulin Rouge, which became some of his most famous works.",
      "date": "1891"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Exhibition at the Goupil & Cie Gallery",
      "text": "In 1893, Toulouse-Lautrec exhibited at the Goupil & Cie Gallery in Paris, gaining significant recognition.",
      "date": "1893"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Health Decline and Institutionalization",
      "text": "In 1899, due to his deteriorating health and alcoholism, Toulouse-Lautrec was institutionalized for several months.",
      "date": "1899"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec",
      "text": "Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec died on September 9, 1901, at the age of 36, at the Château Malromé in Saint-André-du-Bois, France.",
      "date": "1901-09-09"
    }
  ]
}

