      "headline": "Earliest known use of concrete",
      "text": "The earliest known use of concrete dates back to around 6500 BCE in the region of present-day southern Syria and northern Jordan, where the Nabataea traders used a form of concrete to build structures.",
      "date": "-06500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Roman concrete development",
      "text": "By 300 BCE, the Romans had developed a form of concrete that was used extensively in their construction projects, including the Pantheon and the Colosseum.",
      "date": "-0300"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Roman concrete innovation",
      "text": "In 27 BCE, the Romans began using pozzolana, a volcanic ash, to create a hydraulic concrete that could set underwater, allowing them to construct harbors and other structures.",
      "date": "-0027"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rediscovery of concrete",
      "text": "Concrete was largely forgotten after the fall of the Roman Empire until its rediscovery in the 18th century. In 1756, British engineer John Smeaton pioneered the use of hydraulic lime in concrete, which improved its durability.",
      "date": "1756"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Invention of Portland cement",
      "text": "In 1824, Joseph Aspdin, an English bricklayer, patented Portland cement, which became the most common type of cement used in modern concrete.",
      "date": "1824"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First reinforced concrete building",
      "text": "In 1853, François Coignet, a French industrialist, built the first iron-reinforced concrete structure, a four-story house in Paris.",
      "date": "1853"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of reinforced concrete",
      "text": "In 1889, the first reinforced concrete bridge was built in San Francisco, marking a significant advancement in the use of concrete for infrastructure.",
      "date": "1889"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Widespread use of concrete in construction",
      "text": "By the early 20th century, concrete had become a staple in construction, used in buildings, roads, bridges, and dams worldwide.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of pre-stressed concrete",
      "text": "In 1928, Eugène Freyssinet, a French engineer, developed pre-stressed concrete, which allowed for longer spans and more efficient structures.",
      "date": "1928"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of high-performance concrete",
      "text": "In the late 20th century, high-performance concrete was developed, offering greater strength, durability, and resistance to environmental factors.",
      "date": "1980"
    }
  ]
}

