      "headline": "First Pan-African Conference",
      "text": "The first Pan-African Conference was held in London from July 23 to July 25, 1900. It was organized by Trinidadian barrister Henry Sylvester Williams and marked the beginning of the organized Pan-African movement.",
      "date": "1900-07-23"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of the African Association",
      "text": "In 1897, the African Association was formed in London by Henry Sylvester Williams. It aimed to promote and protect the interests of Africans and people of African descent.",
      "date": "1897"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Second Pan-African Congress",
      "text": "The second Pan-African Congress was held in Paris in 1919, organized by W.E.B. Du Bois. It was significant for its call for African self-determination and the end of colonial rule.",
      "date": "1919"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Fifth Pan-African Congress",
      "text": "Held in Manchester, United Kingdom, from October 15 to October 21, 1945, the fifth Pan-African Congress was a significant event that called for the decolonization of Africa and the Caribbean.",
      "date": "1945-10-15"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of the Organization of African Unity",
      "text": "On May 25, 1963, the Organization of African Unity (OAU) was established in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia. It aimed to promote unity and cooperation among African states and was a significant milestone for Pan-Africanism.",
      "date": "1963-05-25"
    },
    {
      "headline": "African Union replaces OAU",
      "text": "In 2001, the African Union (AU) was established in Durban, South Africa, replacing the Organization of African Unity. The AU aimed to accelerate the process of integration in the continent.",
      "date": "2001"
    }
  ]
}

