      "headline": "First synthesis of Mustard Gas",
      "text": "Mustard gas was first synthesized by Frederick Guthrie in 1860. He noted its blistering properties upon exposure to the skin.",
      "date": "1860"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Mustard Gas as a Chemical Weapon",
      "text": "In 1916, during World War I, mustard gas was developed as a chemical weapon by the German army. It was first used in warfare in July 1917 at the Battle of Ypres.",
      "date": "1917-07"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Use in World War I",
      "text": "Mustard gas was used extensively during World War I, causing severe injuries and deaths among soldiers. Its use marked a significant development in chemical warfare.",
      "date": "1917"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Geneva Protocol",
      "text": "In 1925, the Geneva Protocol was signed, prohibiting the use of chemical and biological weapons in war. This was a direct response to the horrors of chemical warfare experienced during World War I.",
      "date": "1925"
    },
    {
      "headline": "World War II Research and Use",
      "text": "During World War II, research into mustard gas continued, although it was not used extensively in combat. Both the Allies and Axis powers stockpiled chemical weapons, including mustard gas.",
      "date": "1939"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Post-World War II Disposal",
      "text": "After World War II, large quantities of mustard gas were disposed of, often by dumping them into the ocean. This has led to environmental concerns and ongoing efforts to safely manage and dispose of chemical weapons.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Chemical Weapons Convention",
      "text": "In 1993, the Chemical Weapons Convention was signed, which aimed to eliminate chemical weapons, including mustard gas, and prevent their use in warfare.",
      "date": "1993"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Destruction of Stockpiles",
      "text": "Following the Chemical Weapons Convention, countries began the process of destroying their stockpiles of mustard gas and other chemical weapons. This process is ongoing, with significant progress made in reducing global stockpiles.",
      "date": "1997"
    }
  ]
}

