      "text": "The ten-codes were developed in 1937-1940 by Charles Hopper, communications director for the Illinois State Police, to allow for brevity and clarity in radio communications.",
      "date": "1937"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adoption by APCO",
      "text": "The Association of Public-Safety Communications Officials-International (APCO) adopted the ten-codes in 1940 as part of their Project 14 to standardize radio communications.",
      "date": "1940"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Widespread Use",
      "text": "Throughout the mid-20th century, ten-codes became widely used by police and emergency services across the United States, becoming a staple in public safety communications.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Criticism and Decline",
      "text": "In the early 2000s, ten-codes began to face criticism for lack of standardization across different agencies, leading to confusion during multi-agency responses.",
      "date": "2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "NIMS Recommendation",
      "text": "In 2005, the National Incident Management System (NIMS) recommended phasing out ten-codes in favor of plain language to improve communication clarity during emergencies.",
      "date": "2005"
    }
  ]
}

