      "text": "The Congress of Vienna officially opened in September 1814. It was a series of diplomatic meetings held to negotiate and establish a new balance of power in Europe following the defeat of Napoleon Bonaparte.",
      "date": "1814-09"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Napoleon's Escape from Elba",
      "text": "On February 26, 1815, Napoleon Bonaparte escaped from his exile on the island of Elba, which caused a significant disruption to the Congress of Vienna as European powers had to address the renewed threat posed by Napoleon.",
      "date": "1815-02-26"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Signing of the Final Act",
      "text": "The Final Act of the Congress of Vienna was signed on June 9, 1815. This document laid out the new political boundaries and balance of power in Europe, aiming to prevent future large-scale conflicts.",
      "date": "1815-06-09"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of Waterloo",
      "text": "On June 18, 1815, the Battle of Waterloo took place, marking the final defeat of Napoleon Bonaparte. This event confirmed the decisions made at the Congress of Vienna and solidified the new European order.",
      "date": "1815-06-18"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Closure of the Congress of Vienna",
      "text": "The Congress of Vienna concluded in June 1815, having successfully established a framework for European peace and stability that would last for several decades.",
      "date": "1815-06"
    }
  ]
}

