    {
      "headline": "First Recorded Case of Decompression Sickness",
      "text": "In 1841, the first recorded case of decompression sickness occurred. It was observed in workers constructing the Hell Gate Bridge in New York City, who were working in pressurized environments.",
      "date": "1841"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Description by Paul Bert",
      "text": "In 1878, French physiologist Paul Bert described decompression sickness in detail. He identified the cause as the formation of nitrogen bubbles in the blood and tissues due to rapid decompression.",
      "date": "1878"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Decompression Tables",
      "text": "In 1908, John Scott Haldane developed the first decompression tables for the Royal Navy, which were based on his research into decompression sickness and aimed to prevent it by controlling ascent rates.",
      "date": "1908"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Hyperbaric Oxygen Therapy",
      "text": "In the mid-20th century, hyperbaric oxygen therapy was introduced as a treatment for decompression sickness. This therapy involves breathing pure oxygen in a pressurized room or chamber.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of the Undersea and Hyperbaric Medical Society",
      "text": "In 1967, the Undersea and Hyperbaric Medical Society was formed to promote research and education in the field of hyperbaric medicine, including decompression sickness.",
      "date": "1967"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Dive Computers",
      "text": "In the late 20th century, dive computers were developed to help divers monitor their depth and time underwater, reducing the risk of decompression sickness by providing real-time data for safer ascents.",
      "date": "1980"
    }
  ]
}

