      "headline": "Founding of Munsey's Weekly",
      "text": "Munsey's Magazine was founded by Frank Munsey in 1889 as Munsey's Weekly, a humor magazine edited by John Kendrick Bangs.",
      "date": "1889"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Financial Loss and Rebranding",
      "text": "By late 1891, Munsey's Weekly had lost $100,000. Munsey converted it into an illustrated general monthly in October of that year, retitled Munsey's Magazine and priced at twenty-five cents.",
      "date": "1891-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Richard Titherington Becomes Editor",
      "text": "Richard Titherington became the editor of Munsey's Magazine in 1891 and remained in that role throughout the magazine's existence.",
      "date": "1891"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Price Reduction to Ten Cents",
      "text": "In 1893, Munsey cut the price of the magazine to ten cents, which brought him into conflict with the American News Company.",
      "date": "1893"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of Own Distribution Company",
      "text": "Munsey started his own distribution company after the American News Company refused to handle the magazine at the reduced price. The first ten cent issue began with a print run of 20,000 copies but eventually sold 60,000, and within a year circulation had risen to over a quarter of a million copies.",
      "date": "1893"
    }
  ]
}

