      "headline": "Origin of the Cat o' Nine Tails",
      "text": "The cat o' nine tails originated as an implement for physical punishment, particularly in the Royal Navy and British Army. Its exact date of origin is not clearly documented, but it was used extensively in the 17th and 18th centuries.",
      "date": "1600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Use in the Royal Navy",
      "text": "The cat o' nine tails was used as a disciplinary tool in the Royal Navy. It was typically made of a short handle with nine knotted cords, used to inflict punishment on sailors for various offenses.",
      "date": "1700"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Judicial Punishment in Britain",
      "text": "The cat o' nine tails was also used as a judicial punishment in Britain, where it was employed to flog criminals as part of their sentence.",
      "date": "1700"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Abolition of Flogging in the British Army",
      "text": "Flogging with the cat o' nine tails was abolished in the British Army in 1881, marking the end of its use as a military punishment.",
      "date": "1881"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Abolition of Flogging in the Royal Navy",
      "text": "The use of the cat o' nine tails in the Royal Navy was abolished in 1881, following the abolition in the British Army.",
      "date": "1881"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Continued Use in Prisons",
      "text": "Despite its abolition in the military, the cat o' nine tails continued to be used in some British prisons until the mid-20th century.",
      "date": "1950"
    }
  ]
}

