      "headline": "Royal Endorsement",
      "text": "In the 1880s, King Kalākaua of Hawaii became a patron of the ukulele, helping to popularize it by including it in royal gatherings and performances.",
      "date": "1880"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ukulele Gains Popularity in the Mainland United States",
      "text": "The ukulele gained widespread popularity in the mainland United States during the Panama-Pacific International Exposition held in San Francisco in 1915, where Hawaiian music was featured prominently.",
      "date": "1915"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ukulele Craze of the 1920s",
      "text": "During the 1920s, the ukulele became a symbol of the Jazz Age, with its popularity soaring across the United States. It was featured in many popular songs and performances of the era.",
      "date": "1920"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decline in Popularity",
      "text": "By the 1930s and 1940s, the popularity of the ukulele began to decline as musical tastes shifted and other instruments became more prominent.",
      "date": "1930"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Revival in the Late 20th Century",
      "text": "The ukulele experienced a revival in the late 20th century, with artists like Israel Kamakawiwo'ole bringing renewed attention to the instrument through his iconic rendition of 'Somewhere Over the Rainbow/What a Wonderful World'.",
      "date": "1990"
