      "headline": "Invention of the Printed Circuit Board",
      "text": "The printed circuit board (PCB) was invented by Paul Eisler, an Austrian engineer, in 1936. He developed the PCB while working on a radio set.",
      "date": "1936"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Commercial Use of PCBs",
      "text": "PCBs were first used commercially in the 1940s. They were initially used in military applications during World War II.",
      "date": "1940"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Double-Sided PCBs",
      "text": "Double-sided PCBs, which allow for more complex circuits, were introduced in the 1950s. This innovation enabled components to be placed on both sides of the board.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Multilayer PCBs",
      "text": "Multilayer PCBs, which consist of multiple layers of conductive material separated by insulating layers, were developed in the 1960s. This advancement allowed for even more complex and compact circuit designs.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Surface Mount Technology",
      "text": "Surface mount technology (SMT) was introduced in the 1980s, allowing components to be mounted directly onto the surface of PCBs. This technology significantly reduced the size and weight of electronic devices.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Advancements in PCB Manufacturing",
      "text": "In the 1990s, advancements in PCB manufacturing techniques, such as the use of computer-aided design (CAD) and automated assembly processes, improved the precision and efficiency of PCB production.",
      "date": "1990"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Environmental Regulations and Lead-Free PCBs",
      "text": "In the early 2000s, environmental regulations led to the development of lead-free PCBs. The Restriction of Hazardous Substances Directive (RoHS) in the European Union, implemented in 2006, was a significant driver of this change.",
      "date": "2006"
    }
  ]
}

{
