    {
      "headline": "Composition of 'Layla'",
      "text": "The song 'Layla' was composed by Eric Clapton and Jim Gordon. Clapton wrote the song inspired by his unrequited love for Pattie Boyd, while Gordon composed the piano coda. The composition process took place in 1970.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Controversy over Piano Part",
      "text": "The piano part of 'Layla' has been controversially credited to Rita Coolidge, who was Jim Gordon's girlfriend at the time. This controversy has persisted since the song's release in 1970.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Chart Performance",
      "text": "Upon its release, 'Layla' initially did not chart highly, but it gained popularity over time. It eventually became a classic rock staple and is considered one of the greatest rock songs of all time.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Re-release and Chart Success",
      "text": "In 1972, 'Layla' was re-released as a single and achieved greater commercial success, reaching number 10 on the Billboard Hot 100 chart.",
      "date": "1972"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Acoustic Version Release",
      "text": "Eric Clapton released an acoustic version of 'Layla' on his 'Unplugged' album in 1992. This version won the Grammy Award for Best Rock Song.",
      "date": "1992"
    }
  ]
}

