      "headline": "Alfred Blalock's Birth",
      "text": "Alfred Blalock was born on April 5, 1899. He was a white American surgeon known for his research on the medical condition of shock and for pioneering modern heart surgery.",
      "date": "1899-04-05"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Vivien Thomas and Alfred Blalock Begin Collaboration",
      "text": "In 1930, Vivien Thomas began working with Alfred Blalock at Vanderbilt University. Their collaboration would lead to significant medical breakthroughs.",
      "date": "1930"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Move to Johns Hopkins Hospital",
      "text": "In 1941, Vivien Thomas and Alfred Blalock moved to Johns Hopkins Hospital in Baltimore, where they continued their groundbreaking work in cardiac surgery.",
      "date": "1941"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Successful Blue Baby Surgery",
      "text": "On November 29, 1944, the first successful 'blue baby' surgery was performed by Alfred Blalock, with significant contributions from Vivien Thomas. This surgery marked a major advancement in the treatment of congenital heart defects.",
      "date": "1944-11-29"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Alfred Blalock's Death",
      "text": "Alfred Blalock passed away on September 15, 1964. He left behind a legacy of pioneering work in heart surgery.",
      "date": "1964-09-15"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Vivien Thomas' Death",
      "text": "Vivien Thomas died on November 26, 1985. His contributions to cardiac surgery were recognized posthumously, and he is remembered as a pioneer in the field.",
      "date": "1985-11-26"
    }
  ]
}

