      "headline": "Outbreak of Anti-Sikh Riots",
      "text": "Following the assassination of Indira Gandhi, anti-Sikh riots broke out in Delhi and other parts of India, beginning on October 31, 1984.",
      "date": "1984-10-31"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peak of Violence",
      "text": "The violence reached its peak between November 1 and November 4, 1984, with widespread killings, looting, and destruction of Sikh properties.",
      "date": "1984-11-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Government Response",
      "text": "The Indian government deployed the army and imposed curfews in an attempt to control the violence, but the response was criticized as being delayed and inadequate.",
      "date": "1984-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of Riots",
      "text": "By November 3, 1984, the violence began to subside as the army took control of the situation.",
      "date": "1984-11-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Official Death Toll",
      "text": "The Indian government reported that approximately 2,800 Sikhs were killed in Delhi and 3,350 nationwide during the riots.",
      "date": "1984"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Aftermath and Investigations",
      "text": "In the years following the riots, numerous commissions and committees were set up to investigate the events, but justice for the victims was widely seen as inadequate.",
      "date": "1984-1985"
    }
  ]
