      "headline": "Development of Submachine Gun",
      "text": "The submachine gun was developed during World War I (1914–1918) as a close quarter offensive weapon, mainly for trench raiding.",
      "date": "1918"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Coining of the Term 'Submachine Gun'",
      "text": "The term 'submachine gun' was coined by John T. Thompson, the inventor of the Thompson submachine gun, to describe its design concept as an automatic firearm with notably less firepower than a machine gun.",
      "date": "1919"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peak Production During World War II",
      "text": "At its peak during World War II (1939–1945), millions of submachine guns were made for assault troops and auxiliaries whose doctrines emphasized close-quarter suppressive fire.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Cold War Era Innovations",
      "text": "New submachine gun designs appeared frequently during the Cold War, especially among special forces, covert operation commandos and mechanized infantrymen.",
      "date": "1947"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decline in Frontline Combat Use",
      "text": "Submachine gun usage for frontline combat decreased in the 1980s and 1990s, as they were largely replaced by assault rifles.",
      "date": "1990"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Continued Use in Security and Law Enforcement",
      "text": "By the early 21st century, submachine guns are still used by security forces, police tactical units, paramilitary and bodyguards for close-quarters combat because they are 'a pistol-caliber weapon that's easy to control, and less likely to overpenetrate the target'.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

