      "headline": "Charles Edward Stuart Lands in Scotland",
      "text": "On July 23, 1745, Charles Edward Stuart, also known as Bonnie Prince Charlie, landed on the island of Eriskay in the Outer Hebrides, Scotland, marking the beginning of the Jacobite rising of 1745.",
      "date": "1745-07-23"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Raising of the Jacobite Standard",
      "text": "On August 19, 1745, Charles Edward Stuart raised the Jacobite standard at Glenfinnan, officially starting the rebellion.",
      "date": "1745-08-19"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of Prestonpans",
      "text": "On September 21, 1745, the Jacobite forces won a significant victory at the Battle of Prestonpans against the government army led by Sir John Cope.",
      "date": "1745-09-21"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Jacobites Enter Edinburgh",
      "text": "In September 1745, following their victory at Prestonpans, the Jacobites entered Edinburgh and Charles Edward Stuart was declared regent.",
      "date": "1745-09"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Invasion of England",
      "text": "In November 1745, the Jacobite army crossed into England, advancing as far south as Derby by December 4, 1745.",
      "date": "1745-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Retreat from Derby",
      "text": "On December 6, 1745, the Jacobite forces decided to retreat from Derby and return to Scotland, realizing they lacked sufficient support to continue their advance.",
      "date": "1745-12-06"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of Falkirk Muir",
      "text": "On January 17, 1746, the Jacobites won another victory at the Battle of Falkirk Muir against government forces.",
      "date": "1746-01-17"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of Culloden",
      "text": "On April 16, 1746, the Jacobite forces were decisively defeated by the British Army at the Battle of Culloden, effectively ending the rising.",
      "date": "1746-04-16"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Charles Edward Stuart Escapes",
      "text": "Following the defeat at Culloden, Charles Edward Stuart went into hiding and eventually escaped to France in September 1746.",
      "date": "1746-09"
    }
  ]
}

