      "text": "In 1897, Hastings Gilford, another British doctor, further studied the condition and published additional findings, leading to the syndrome being named Hutchinson-Gilford Progeria Syndrome.",
      "date": "1897"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Identification of Genetic Mutation",
      "text": "In 2003, researchers identified the genetic mutation responsible for Progeria. The mutation occurs in the LMNA gene, which produces an abnormal protein called progerin.",
      "date": "2003"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Lonafarnib",
      "text": "In the early 2000s, the drug Lonafarnib was developed and tested as a treatment for Progeria. It showed promise in improving some symptoms and extending the lifespan of affected individuals.",
      "date": "2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "FDA Approval of Lonafarnib",
      "text": "On November 20, 2020, the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approved Lonafarnib (Zokinvy) as the first treatment for Hutchinson-Gilford Progeria Syndrome.",
      "date": "2020-11-20"
    }
  ]
}

