      "headline": "Formation of the Contras",
      "text": "The Contras were formed in 1979 as right-wing militias to wage anti-communist guerilla warfare against the Marxist régime of the Sandinista National Liberation Front in Nicaragua.",
      "date": "1979"
    },
    {
      "headline": "U.S. Support for the Contras",
      "text": "In the early 1980s, the United States began providing support to the Contras, including funding and training, as part of its efforts to counter the spread of communism in Central America.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Boland Amendment",
      "text": "In 1982, the U.S. Congress passed the Boland Amendment, which restricted U.S. government assistance to the Contras, leading to covert operations to continue support.",
      "date": "1982"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Iran-Contra Affair",
      "text": "The Iran-Contra Affair was a political scandal in the United States that came to light in 1986, revealing that senior officials secretly facilitated the sale of arms to Iran, with proceeds being used to fund the Contras.",
      "date": "1986"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of the Nicaraguan Resistance",
      "text": "By 1987, the CIA had organized most of the Contra militias into the anti-communist Nicaraguan Resistance, with the Nicaraguan Democratic Force (FDN) being the largest militia.",
      "date": "1987"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of Contra War",
      "text": "The Contra war effectively ended in 1990 after the Sandinistas lost the general election to Violeta Chamorro, leading to a peace agreement and the disbandment of the Contras.",
      "date": "1990"
    }
  ]
}

