      "headline": "Emergence of Heathenry",
      "text": "Heathenry began to emerge as a distinct religious movement in the early 20th century. It was developed in Europe as practitioners sought to reconstruct the pre-Christian religions of the Germanic peoples.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Early Heathen Organizations",
      "text": "The first Heathen organizations began to form in the early to mid-20th century. These groups were often small and localized, focusing on reviving ancient Germanic religious practices.",
      "date": "1930"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Post-War Revival",
      "text": "After World War II, there was a renewed interest in Heathenry, particularly in the 1960s and 1970s, as part of a broader revival of interest in Pagan and indigenous European spiritualities.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of Ásatrúarfélagið",
      "text": "In 1972, Ásatrúarfélagið, one of the first legally recognized Heathen organizations, was founded in Iceland. It played a significant role in the modern Heathen movement.",
      "date": "1972"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Growth in the United States",
      "text": "During the late 20th century, Heathenry began to grow in the United States, with the establishment of various organizations and communities dedicated to the practice.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "International Expansion",
      "text": "By the early 21st century, Heathenry had expanded internationally, with communities and organizations established in many countries across Europe, North America, and beyond.",
      "date": "2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Recognition in Iceland",
      "text": "In 2017, the Icelandic government granted Ásatrúarfélagið permission to build a temple, marking a significant recognition of Heathenry as a legitimate religious movement.",
      "date": "2017"
    }
  ]
}

