      "text": "The Mycenaean Greek period is the earliest attested phase of the Greek language, used from around 1500 BCE to 1200 BCE. It is primarily known from inscriptions in Linear B script found on clay tablets.",
      "date": "-1500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of Mycenaean Greek Period",
      "text": "The Mycenaean Greek period came to an end around 1200 BCE, coinciding with the collapse of Mycenaean civilization.",
      "date": "-1200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Greek Dark Ages",
      "text": "Following the collapse of the Mycenaean civilization, the Greek Dark Ages began around 1200 BCE and lasted until approximately 800 BCE. During this time, there was a decline in the use of writing.",
      "date": "-1200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Archaic or Homeric Period",
      "text": "The Archaic or Homeric period of the Greek language began around 800 BCE and lasted until approximately 500 BCE. This period saw the development of the Greek alphabet and the composition of the Homeric epics.",
      "date": "-0800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Classical Greek Period",
      "text": "The Classical Greek period spanned from around 500 BCE to 300 BCE. This era is marked by the flourishing of Greek literature, philosophy, and the arts, with figures such as Plato and Aristotle contributing to its legacy.",
      "date": "-0500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of Classical Greek Period",
      "text": "The Classical Greek period ended around 300 BCE, transitioning into the Hellenistic period following the conquests of Alexander the Great.",
      "date": "-0300"
    }
  ]
}

