      "headline": "Origins of the Novel",
      "text": "The novel has a continuous and comprehensive history of about two thousand years, with its origins in the Ancient Greek and Roman novel.",
      "date": "-0001"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Medieval Chivalric Romance",
      "text": "The tradition of the novel was influenced by Medieval Chivalric romance, which was popular during the Middle Ages.",
      "date": "1100"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Italian Renaissance Novella",
      "text": "The Italian Renaissance novella contributed to the development of the novel, with its focus on new and short stories.",
      "date": "1300"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Revival by Romanticism",
      "text": "The ancient romance form was revived by Romanticism, particularly in the historical romances of Walter Scott and the Gothic novel.",
      "date": "1800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Realism in Novels",
      "text": "M. H. Abrams and Walter Scott argued that a novel is a fiction narrative that displays a realistic depiction of the state of a society.",
      "date": "1800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Romance vs. Novel",
      "text": "Some novelists, including Nathaniel Hawthorne, Herman Melville, Ann Radcliffe, and John Cowper Powys, preferred the term 'romance' for their works, which should not be confused with the genre fiction romance novel.",
      "date": "1850"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modern Novels with Marvellous Incidents",
      "text": "Works of fiction that include marvellous or uncommon incidents are also commonly called novels, such as Mary Shelley's Frankenstein, J. R. R. Tolkien's The Lord of the Rings, and Harper Lee's To Kill a Mockingbird.",
      "date": "1900"
    }
  ]
}

