      "headline": "1968 Democratic National Convention Protests",
      "text": "In August 1968, during the Democratic National Convention in Chicago, Illinois, large-scale protests against the Vietnam War and the political status quo took place. These protests were marked by significant clashes between demonstrators and police.",
      "date": "1968-08"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Indictment of the Chicago Eight",
      "text": "In March 1969, the United States Department of Justice indicted eight individuals, known as the Chicago Eight, on charges of conspiracy and inciting a riot related to the protests during the 1968 Democratic National Convention.",
      "date": "1969-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Trial of the Chicago Eight Begins",
      "text": "The trial of the Chicago Eight began on September 24, 1969, in the United States District Court for the Northern District of Illinois.",
      "date": "1969-09-24"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bobby Seale's Mistrial",
      "text": "On November 5, 1969, Judge Julius Hoffman declared a mistrial for Bobby Seale, one of the original defendants, after he was bound and gagged in the courtroom. This reduced the group to the Chicago Seven.",
      "date": "1969-11-05"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Verdict of the Chicago Seven",
      "text": "On February 18, 1970, the jury acquitted all seven defendants of conspiracy, but five were convicted of crossing state lines with intent to incite a riot. They were sentenced to five years in prison and fined $5,000 each.",
      "date": "1970-02-18"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Appeal and Reversal of Convictions",
      "text": "On November 21, 1972, the United States Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit reversed the convictions of the Chicago Seven, citing errors by the trial judge and bias in jury selection.",
      "date": "1972-11-21"
    }
  ]
}

