      "headline": "Emergence of the 996 Working Hour System",
      "text": "The 996 working hour system began to emerge in the early 21st century, particularly within China's tech industry. It refers to a work schedule of 9:00 am to 9:00 pm, six days a week.",
      "date": "2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Widespread Adoption in Tech Companies",
      "text": "By the 2010s, the 996 working hour system became widely adopted by many Chinese internet and tech companies as their official or de-facto work schedule.",
      "date": "2010"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Criticism and Controversy",
      "text": "The 996 working hour system faced significant criticism for violating the Labour Law of the People's Republic of China, which mandates a maximum 44-hour workweek. Critics labeled it as 'modern slavery'.",
      "date": "2016"
    },
    {
      "headline": "996.ICU Movement",
      "text": "In March 2019, the 996.ICU movement was launched on GitHub, highlighting the harsh working conditions and advocating for workers' rights. The name '996.ICU' suggests that employees working under the 996 system are at risk of ending up in an Intensive Care Unit.",
      "date": "2019-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Public and Government Response",
      "text": "Following the 996.ICU movement, the Chinese government and media began to address the issue more openly, with some companies pledging to improve working conditions.",
      "date": "2019"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Legal Actions and Reforms",
      "text": "In August 2021, China's Supreme People's Court and Ministry of Human Resources and Social Security released guidelines declaring the 996 work schedule illegal, reinforcing labor laws and workers' rights.",
      "date": "2021-08"
    }
  ]
}

