      "headline": "Invention of Lithography",
      "text": "Lithography was invented in 1796 by the German author and actor Alois Senefelder. It is a planographic method of printing based on the immiscibility of oil and water.",
      "date": "1796"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Early Uses of Lithography",
      "text": "Initially, lithography was used mostly for printing musical scores and maps. The technique allowed for high-quality reproductions of detailed images and text.",
      "date": "1796"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Chromolithography",
      "text": "In the 19th century, chromolithography, a method for producing multi-color prints, was developed. This expanded the artistic and commercial applications of lithography.",
      "date": "1800s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Advancements in Lithographic Technology",
      "text": "Throughout the 19th and 20th centuries, lithographic technology continued to advance, leading to the development of offset lithography, which became a dominant method for commercial printing.",
      "date": "1900s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modern Commercial Lithography",
      "text": "Today, lithography is widely used in commercial printing, including the production of books, posters, packaging, and more. Modern lithography has evolved to include digital processes.",
      "date": "2000s"
    }
  ]
}

