      "headline": "1906 San Francisco Earthquake Occurs",
      "text": "On April 18, 1906, at 05:12 AM Pacific Standard Time, a major earthquake with an estimated moment magnitude of 7.9 struck the coast of Northern California. The earthquake had a maximum Mercalli intensity of XI (Extreme).",
      "date": "1906-04-18"
    },
    {
      "headline": "High-Intensity Shaking Felt",
      "text": "The earthquake caused high-intensity shaking that was felt from Eureka on the North Coast to the Salinas Valley, an agricultural region to the south of the San Francisco Bay Area.",
      "date": "1906-04-18"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Devastating Fires Break Out",
      "text": "Following the earthquake, devastating fires broke out in San Francisco and lasted for several days, contributing significantly to the destruction.",
      "date": "1906-04-18"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Massive Destruction in San Francisco",
      "text": "More than 80% of San Francisco was destroyed due to the earthquake and subsequent fires.",
      "date": "1906-04-18"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death Toll and Impact",
      "text": "The earthquake resulted in more than 3,000 deaths, making it the deadliest earthquake in the history of the United States and the greatest loss of life from a natural disaster in California's history.",
      "date": "1906-04-18"
    }
  ]
}

