      "headline": "Existence of Homo habilis",
      "text": "Homo habilis existed approximately from 2.3 million years ago to 1.65 million years ago during the Early Pleistocene in East and South Africa.",
      "date": "-2300000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Discovery and Naming",
      "text": "Homo habilis was first described and named in 1964. The species was initially contested, with some researchers suggesting it should be synonymized with Australopithecus africanus.",
      "date": "1964"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Recognition as a Human Ancestor",
      "text": "By the 1980s, Homo habilis was proposed to have been a direct ancestor of Homo erectus, which in turn led to modern humans. This view has since been debated.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Debate on Species Classification",
      "text": "Several specimens with insecure species identification were assigned to Homo habilis, leading to arguments for splitting into 'H. rudolfensis' and 'H. gautengensis'. Only 'H. rudolfensis' has received wide support.",
      "date": "1980"
    }
  ]
}

