      "headline": "Early Use of Chi Rho",
      "text": "The Chi Rho symbol was used by early Christians as a symbol of Christ. It is formed by superimposing the first two letters of the Greek word for Christ, ΧΡΙΣΤΟΣ.",
      "date": "-0033"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Constantine's Vision",
      "text": "In 312, before the Battle of the Milvian Bridge, Emperor Constantine reportedly had a vision of the Chi Rho symbol with the words 'In this sign, you will conquer'.",
      "date": "0312"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of the Milvian Bridge",
      "text": "On October 28, 312, Constantine's forces won the Battle of the Milvian Bridge, after which he adopted the Chi Rho as a military standard.",
      "date": "0312-10-28"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Edict of Milan",
      "text": "In 313, Constantine issued the Edict of Milan, which granted religious tolerance throughout the Roman Empire and allowed the open practice of Christianity.",
      "date": "0313"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Council of Nicaea",
      "text": "In 325, the First Council of Nicaea was convened by Constantine, marking a significant moment in the establishment of Christian orthodoxy, where the Chi Rho continued to be a prominent symbol.",
      "date": "0325"
    }
  ]
}

{
