      "headline": "Stonewall Riots",
      "text": "The Stonewall riots began on June 28, 1969, in New York City. These were a series of spontaneous demonstrations by members of the LGBTQ community in response to a police raid at the Stonewall Inn, a gay bar in Greenwich Village. The riots are widely considered to be a catalyst for the modern LGBTQ rights movement.",
      "date": "1969-06-28"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Pride March",
      "text": "On June 28, 1970, the first Pride march took place in New York City, commemorating the one-year anniversary of the Stonewall riots. This event marked the beginning of annual Pride celebrations.",
      "date": "1970-06-28"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of Pride Month",
      "text": "In 1999, President Bill Clinton officially declared June as Gay and Lesbian Pride Month in the United States, recognizing the impact of LGBTQ individuals on society.",
      "date": "1999"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Expansion to LGBTQ Pride Month",
      "text": "In 2009, President Barack Obama expanded the observance to include the entire LGBTQ community, officially proclaiming June as Lesbian, Gay, Bisexual, and Transgender Pride Month.",
      "date": "2009"
    },
    {
      "headline": "50th Anniversary of Stonewall",
      "text": "In June 2019, the 50th anniversary of the Stonewall riots was celebrated with numerous events and parades worldwide, highlighting the progress made in LGBTQ rights over the past five decades.",
      "date": "2019-06"
    }
  ]
}

