{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Invention of Gunpowder",
      "text": "Gunpowder was invented in China during the Tang dynasty (618–907 AD). It was initially used for fireworks and signals, but its potential for military applications was soon realized.",
      "date": "0900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Recorded Use of Gunpowder Artillery",
      "text": "The first recorded use of gunpowder artillery in warfare was during the Siege of Seville in 1248, where the Moors used it against the Spanish.",
      "date": "1248"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Bombards",
      "text": "Bombards, large-caliber, muzzle-loading artillery pieces, were used in Europe during the 14th century. They were primarily used in sieges to breach walls.",
      "date": "1300"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Field Artillery",
      "text": "By the 15th century, lighter and more mobile field artillery began to be developed, allowing for greater tactical flexibility on the battlefield.",
      "date": "1400"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Great Turkish Bombard",
      "text": "In 1453, the Ottoman Empire used massive bombards to breach the walls of Constantinople, leading to the city's fall and marking a significant advancement in siege artillery.",
      "date": "1453"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Cannon",
      "text": "Cannons became more widespread in the 16th century, with improvements in metallurgy and gunpowder allowing for more powerful and reliable artillery pieces.",
      "date": "1500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Napoleonic Wars and Artillery Advancements",
      "text": "During the Napoleonic Wars (1803-1815), artillery tactics and technology advanced significantly, with the introduction of horse artillery and the use of artillery in massed batteries.",
      "date": "1803"
    },
    {
