      "headline": "Chinese Civil War and ROC Retreat",
      "text": "In 1949, after being defeated by the Chinese Communist Party (CCP) during the Chinese Civil War, the Republic of China (ROC) government retreated to Taiwan.",
      "date": "1949"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of the People's Republic of China",
      "text": "On October 1, 1949, the People's Republic of China (PRC) was established by the Chinese Communist Party, marking the beginning of the PRC's claim over all of China, including Taiwan.",
      "date": "1949-10-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "ROC's Limited Jurisdiction",
      "text": "Since 1949, the effective jurisdiction of the ROC has been limited to Taiwan, Penghu, Kinmen, Matsu, and smaller islands.",
      "date": "1949"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Taiwan's Expulsion from the United Nations",
      "text": "On October 25, 1971, the United Nations General Assembly passed Resolution 2758, which recognized the People's Republic of China as the only legitimate representative of China to the United Nations, effectively expelling the ROC.",
      "date": "1971-10-25"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Taiwan Relations Act",
      "text": "On April 10, 1979, the United States enacted the Taiwan Relations Act, which defined the non-diplomatic relations between the US and Taiwan after the US recognized the PRC.",
      "date": "1979-04-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Direct Presidential Election in Taiwan",
      "text": "In 1996, Taiwan held its first direct presidential election, marking a significant step in its democratic development.",
      "date": "1996"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Cross-Strait Relations Act",
      "text": "In 1992, the Cross-Strait Relations Act was enacted by Taiwan to manage relations with the PRC, including economic and cultural exchanges.",
      "date": "1992"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Chen Shui-bian's Presidency",
      "text": "In 2000, Chen Shui-bian was elected President of Taiwan, representing the Democratic Progressive Party, which advocates for Taiwanese independence.",
      "date": "2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Anti-Secession Law",
      "text": "On March 14, 2005, the PRC passed the Anti-Secession Law, which authorizes the use of force against Taiwan if it formally declares independence.",
      "date": "2005-03-14"
    },
