      "headline": "Earliest Celebrations of Lunar New Year",
      "text": "The Lunar New Year has been celebrated for thousands of years, with its origins tracing back to ancient China. It is believed to have started during the Shang Dynasty (c. 1600–1046 BCE) as a way to honor deities and ancestors at the beginning of the year.",
      "date": "-1600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Han Dynasty Formalization",
      "text": "During the Han Dynasty (202 BCE–220 CE), the celebration of the Lunar New Year became more formalized. It was during this period that many of the traditional customs and rituals associated with the festival were established.",
      "date": "-0202"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Zodiac",
      "text": "The Chinese zodiac, which assigns an animal to each year in a 12-year cycle, was introduced during the Han Dynasty. This system became an integral part of the Lunar New Year celebrations.",
      "date": "-0202"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Tang Dynasty Celebrations",
      "text": "The Tang Dynasty (618–907 CE) saw the Lunar New Year become a major festival with elaborate celebrations, including fireworks, dragon dances, and lantern displays.",
      "date": "0618"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Spread to Other Cultures",
      "text": "Over the centuries, the celebration of the Lunar New Year spread to other East Asian cultures, including Korea, Vietnam, and Mongolia, each incorporating their own customs and traditions.",
      "date": "1000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modern Celebrations",
      "text": "In modern times, the Lunar New Year is celebrated by millions of people around the world. It is a public holiday in many countries and is marked by family reunions, feasts, and various cultural performances.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

