      "headline": "Cournot's Model of Competition",
      "text": "In 1838, Antoine Augustin Cournot introduced the concept of Nash equilibrium in his model of competition in an oligopoly. This was one of the earliest uses of the concept, although it was not yet formally defined as a Nash equilibrium.",
      "date": "1838"
    },
    {
      "headline": "John Nash's Contribution",
      "text": "In 1950, John Nash formally defined the concept of Nash equilibrium in his doctoral dissertation. He demonstrated that every finite game has an equilibrium point, where no player can benefit by changing their strategy while the other players keep theirs unchanged.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of Nash's Paper",
      "text": "In 1951, John Nash published his paper 'Non-Cooperative Games' in the journal 'Annals of Mathematics'. This paper laid the foundation for the formal study of Nash equilibrium in game theory.",
      "date": "1951"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Nobel Prize in Economic Sciences",
      "text": "In 1994, John Nash was awarded the Nobel Prize in Economic Sciences, along with John Harsanyi and Reinhard Selten, for their pioneering analysis of equilibria in the theory of non-cooperative games.",
      "date": "1994"
    }
  ]
}

