      "text": "The brit milah, or bris, became a central ritual in Jewish religious practice, signifying the covenant between God and the Jewish people. It is traditionally performed on the eighth day after a male infant's birth.",
      "date": "0001"  },
    {
      "headline": "Role of the Mohel",
      "text": "The role of the mohel, a person trained to perform the circumcision, became established as a critical part of the brit milah ceremony.",
      "date": "1000"  },
    {
      "headline": "Brit Milah in Samaritanism",
      "text": "The practice of brit milah is also observed in Samaritanism, reflecting its shared roots with Judaism.",
      "date": "1000"  },
    {
      "headline": "Modern Brit Milah Practices",
      "text": "Today, the brit milah is generally performed by a mohel on the eighth day after the infant's birth and is followed by a celebratory meal known as seudat mitzvah.",
      "date": "2023"  }
  ]
}

