      "headline": "Discovery of Piezoelectricity",
      "text": "In 1880, French physicists Jacques and Pierre Curie discovered the piezoelectric effect. They found that certain crystals, such as quartz, generate an electric charge in response to mechanical stress.",
      "date": "1880"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Coining of the Term 'Piezoelectricity'",
      "text": "The term 'piezoelectricity' was coined in 1881 by the German physicist Wilhelm Gottlieb Hankel. It is derived from the Greek words 'piezein', meaning to squeeze or press, and 'elektron', meaning amber.",
      "date": "1881"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Term in English",
      "text": "The English term 'piezoelectricity' was introduced in 1883, following the German form 'Piezoelektricität'.",
      "date": "1883"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of the First Piezoelectric Devices",
      "text": "In the early 20th century, piezoelectric materials began to be used in practical applications, such as sonar devices during World War I.",
      "date": "1910"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Advancements in Piezoelectric Materials",
      "text": "Throughout the 20th century, advancements were made in the development of synthetic piezoelectric materials, such as barium titanate and lead zirconate titanate, which expanded the range of applications.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modern Applications of Piezoelectricity",
      "text": "Today, piezoelectric materials are used in a wide range of applications, including medical ultrasound equipment, electronic frequency generation, and as sensors in various industries.",
      "date": "2023"
    }
  ]
}

