      "headline": "Signing of the Antarctic Treaty",
      "text": "The Antarctic Treaty was signed on December 1, 1959, by 12 countries: Argentina, Australia, Belgium, Chile, France, Japan, New Zealand, Norway, South Africa, the United Kingdom, the United States, and the Soviet Union. It was the first arms control agreement established during the Cold War, designating Antarctica as a scientific preserve and banning military activity.",
      "date": "1959-12-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Entry into Force of the Antarctic Treaty",
      "text": "The Antarctic Treaty entered into force on June 23, 1961, after being ratified by the original 12 signatory countries. It established the legal framework for the management of Antarctica.",
      "date": "1961-06-23"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Antarctic Treaty Consultative Meeting",
      "text": "The first Antarctic Treaty Consultative Meeting (ATCM) was held in Canberra, Australia, in 1961. These meetings are held to discuss measures to further the principles and objectives of the Treaty.",
      "date": "1961"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adoption of the Agreed Measures for the Conservation of Antarctic Fauna and Flora",
      "text": "In 1964, the Agreed Measures for the Conservation of Antarctic Fauna and Flora were adopted. These measures were the first set of regulations to protect the Antarctic environment and its ecosystems.",
      "date": "1964"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Convention for the Conservation of Antarctic Seals",
      "text": "The Convention for the Conservation of Antarctic Seals was signed in 1972 and entered into force in 1978. It was established to regulate the sealing industry and ensure the conservation of seal populations.",
      "date": "1972"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Convention on the Conservation of Antarctic Marine Living Resources",
      "text": "The Convention on the Conservation of Antarctic Marine Living Resources (CCAMLR) was signed in 1980 and entered into force in 1982. It aims to preserve marine life and environmental integrity in and near Antarctica.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Protocol on Environmental Protection to the Antarctic Treaty",
      "text": "The Protocol on Environmental Protection to the Antarctic Treaty, also known as the Madrid Protocol, was signed on October 4, 1991, and entered into force on January 14, 1998. It designates Antarctica as a 'natural reserve, devoted to peace and science' and establishes environmental principles for the conduct of all activities.",
      "date": "1991-10-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of the Antarctic Treaty Secretariat",
      "text": "The Antarctic Treaty Secretariat was established in September 2004 to support the annual Antarctic Treaty Consultative Meetings and to facilitate the exchange of information between the Parties. It is headquartered in Buenos Aires, Argentina.",
      "date": "2004-09"
    }
  ]
}

{
