    {
      "headline": "Emergence of Vulgar Latin",
      "text": "Vulgar Latin began to emerge as a distinct form of Latin during the Late Roman Republic. It was the non-formal register of Latin spoken by the common people, differing from the Classical Latin used in literature and formal contexts.",
      "date": "-0100"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Spread of Vulgar Latin",
      "text": "As the Roman Empire expanded, Vulgar Latin spread throughout the territories, adapting to local languages and dialects. This process continued from the 1st century BCE through the 3rd century CE.",
      "date": "0001"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Vulgar Latin in the Roman Empire",
      "text": "During the height of the Roman Empire, Vulgar Latin was the lingua franca across Europe, North Africa, and parts of the Middle East. It was used in everyday communication by soldiers, merchants, and settlers.",
      "date": "0200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Transition to Romance Languages",
      "text": "By the 6th century, Vulgar Latin had evolved significantly in different regions, leading to the development of distinct Romance languages such as Spanish, French, Italian, Portuguese, and Romanian.",
      "date": "0500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Recognition of Vulgar Latin",
      "text": "In the 19th century, scholars like Raynouard began to study Vulgar Latin as a distinct linguistic phenomenon, recognizing its role in the evolution of Romance languages.",
      "date": "1800"
    }
  ]
}

