      "headline": "Swissair Flight 111 Crash",
      "text": "On 2 September 1998, Swissair Flight 111, a McDonnell Douglas MD-11 registered as HB-IWF, crashed into the Atlantic Ocean southwest of Halifax Stanfield International Airport at the entrance to St. Margarets Bay, Nova Scotia, Canada. The crash resulted in the deaths of all 215 passengers and 14 crew members on board, making it the deadliest accident in the history of Swissair and the deadliest accident involving the McDonnell Douglas MD-11.",
      "date": "1998-09-02"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Investigation and Findings",
      "text": "The Transportation Safety Board of Canada conducted an extensive investigation into the crash of Swissair Flight 111. The final report, released on 27 March 2003, concluded that the crash was caused by a fire in the cockpit, which was likely ignited by faulty wiring and spread rapidly due to flammable materials in the aircraft's structure.",
      "date": "2003-03-27"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Memorials Established",
      "text": "In the aftermath of the crash, memorials were established near the crash site in Nova Scotia, Canada, to honor the victims of Swissair Flight 111. These memorials serve as a place of remembrance for the families and friends of those who lost their lives.",
      "date": "1998"
    }
  ]
}

