      "headline": "Existence of Mosasaurus",
      "text": "Mosasaurus lived from about 82 to 66 million years ago during the Campanian and Maastrichtian stages of the Late Cretaceous.",
      "date": "-0082"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Discovery of Mosasaurus Fossils",
      "text": "The first fossils of Mosasaurus were found as skulls in a chalk quarry near the Dutch city of Maastricht in the late 18th century, around 1780. One skull was famously nicknamed the 'great animal of Maastricht'.",
      "date": "1780"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Georges Cuvier's Conclusion",
      "text": "In 1808, naturalist Georges Cuvier concluded that the fossils belonged to a giant marine lizard with similarities to monitor lizards but otherwise unlike any known living animal. This concept was revolutionary at the time and helped support the then-developing ideas of extinction.",
      "date": "1808"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Naming of Mosasaurus",
      "text": "In 1822, William Daniel Conybeare named the genus Mosasaurus in reference to its origin in fossil deposits near the Meuse River.",
      "date": "1822"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Formation of the Pre-Raphaelite Brotherhood",
      "text": "The Pre-Raphaelite Brotherhood was founded in 1848 by William Holman Hunt, John Everett Millais, Dante Gabriel Rossetti, William Michael Rossetti, James Collinson, Frederic George Stephens, and Thomas Woolner. The group was inspired by the Nazarene movement and aimed to reform art by rejecting what they considered the mechanistic approach first adopted by Mannerist artists who succeeded Raphael and Michelangelo.",
      "date": "1848"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Exhibition",
      "text": "In 1849, the Pre-Raphaelite Brotherhood held their first exhibition. The works displayed included Millais's 'Isabella', Rossetti's 'The Girlhood of Mary Virgin', and Hunt's 'Rienzi'. These paintings were marked by their attention to detail, vibrant colors, and complex compositions.",
      "date": "1849"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of The Germ",
      "text": "In January 1850, the Brotherhood began publishing a magazine called 'The Germ', which was intended to promote their ideas and featured poetry, essays, and illustrations. The magazine only ran for four issues.",
      "date": "1850-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Criticism and Controversy",
      "text": "The Brotherhood faced significant criticism from the art establishment and the press, particularly after the 1850 Royal Academy exhibition. Critics attacked their work for its perceived lack of finish and unconventional subject matter.",
      "date": "1850"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Support from John Ruskin",
      "text": "In 1851, art critic John Ruskin came to the defense of the Pre-Raphaelites, praising their attention to nature and truthfulness in art. Ruskin's support helped to legitimize the movement and gain them a wider audience.",
      "date": "1851"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Dissolution of the Brotherhood",
      "text": "By 1853, the original Brotherhood had largely dissolved as members pursued individual careers and interests. However, the influence of their ideas continued to spread and inspire other artists.",
      "date": "1853"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Legacy and Influence",
      "text": "The principles of the Pre-Raphaelite Brotherhood continued to influence artists such as Edward Burne-Jones, William Morris, and John William Waterhouse. Their emphasis on realism, nature, and medieval themes became a significant part of the later Arts and Crafts Movement.",
      "date": "Late 19th century"
    }
  ]
}

