      "headline": "Foundation of the Old Swiss Confederacy",
      "text": "The Old Swiss Confederacy was founded in 1291 with the alliance of the three cantons Uri, Schwyz, and Unterwalden, known as the Waldstätte.",
      "date": "1291"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Expansion to Acht Orte",
      "text": "By 1353, the Old Swiss Confederacy expanded to include five more cantons: Lucerne, Zurich, Glarus, Zug, and Bern, forming the Acht Orte (Eight Cantons).",
      "date": "1353"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Expansion to Dreizehn Orte",
      "text": "Between 1481 and 1513, the confederacy expanded further to include Fribourg, Solothurn, Basel, Schaffhausen, and Appenzell, forming the Dreizehn Orte (Thirteen Cantons).",
      "date": "1513"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Helvetic Republic",
      "text": "In 1798, the Old Swiss Confederacy was replaced by the Helvetic Republic, a centralized state imposed by the French Directory.",
      "date": "1798"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Act of Mediation",
      "text": "In 1803, the Act of Mediation was issued by Napoleon Bonaparte, which restored a confederation of 19 cantons.",
      "date": "1803"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Federal Constitution of 1848",
      "text": "The modern Swiss Confederation was established with the Federal Constitution of 1848, which created a federal state with 25 cantons.",
      "date": "1848"
