      "text": "The 20th century ended on December 31, 2000, marking the conclusion of a century characterized by significant technological, political, and social changes.",
      "date": "2000-12-31"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Founding of Kandahar",
      "text": "Kandahar is believed to have been founded by Alexander the Great in 330 BCE during his conquest of the Persian Empire. The city was originally named Alexandria Arachosia.",
      "date": "-0330"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Kandahar under the Maurya Empire",
      "text": "In the 3rd century BCE, Kandahar became part of the Maurya Empire under Ashoka, who spread Buddhism in the region.",
      "date": "-0300"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Kandahar under the Saffarid Dynasty",
      "text": "In 870 CE, Kandahar was captured by the Saffarid dynasty, marking the beginning of Islamic influence in the region.",
      "date": "0870"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Kandahar under the Ghaznavid Empire",
      "text": "In the 10th century, Kandahar became part of the Ghaznavid Empire, which was known for its cultural and architectural achievements.",
      "date": "1000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Kandahar under the Timurid Empire",
      "text": "In the 14th century, Kandahar came under the control of the Timurid Empire, which was founded by Timur (Tamerlane).",
      "date": "1400"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Kandahar under the Mughal Empire",
      "text": "In 1522, Kandahar was captured by Babur, the founder of the Mughal Empire, and became a significant city within the empire.",
      "date": "1522"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Kandahar under the Safavid Empire",
      "text": "In 1622, Kandahar was taken by the Safavid Empire, marking a period of Persian influence in the city.",
      "date": "1622"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Kandahar under the Hotak Dynasty",
      "text": "In 1709, Mirwais Hotak led a successful rebellion against the Safavids and established the Hotak dynasty in Kandahar.",
      "date": "1709"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Kandahar under the Durrani Empire",
      "text": "In 1747, Ahmad Shah Durrani, the founder of the Durrani Empire, made Kandahar the capital of his new empire.",
      "date": "1747"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Anglo-Afghan War",
      "text": "During the First Anglo-Afghan War (1839-1842), Kandahar was occupied by British forces.",
      "date": "1839"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Second Anglo-Afghan War",
      "text": "In 1878, during the Second Anglo-Afghan War, British forces again occupied Kandahar.",
      "date": "1878"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of Kandahar",
      "text": "On September 1, 1880, the Battle of Kandahar took place, marking a decisive British victory during the Second Anglo-Afghan War.",
      "date": "1880-09-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Soviet-Afghan War",
      "text": "During the Soviet-Afghan War (1979-1989), Kandahar was a major battleground between Soviet forces and Afghan mujahideen.",
      "date": "1979"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Taliban Capture Kandahar",
      "text": "In 1994, the Taliban captured Kandahar, making it their spiritual center and base of operations.",
      "date": "1994"
    },
    {
      "headline": "U.S. Invasion of Afghanistan",
      "text": "In 2001, following the U.S. invasion of Afghanistan, Kandahar was a focal point of military operations against the Taliban.",
      "date": "2001"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Fall of Kandahar to Taliban",
      "text": "In August 2021, Kandahar fell to the Taliban during their rapid takeover of Afghanistan following the withdrawal of U.S. and NATO forces.",
      "date": "2021-08"
    }
  ]
}

