    {
      "headline": "Origin of the Term 'Shell Shock'",
      "text": "The term 'shell shock' was first used in 1915 during World War I to describe the psychological trauma experienced by soldiers exposed to the intense bombardment and fighting.",
      "date": "1915"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Recognition of Shell Shock Symptoms",
      "text": "By 1916, symptoms of shell shock were widely recognized among soldiers, including panic, fear, and an inability to reason, sleep, walk, or talk.",
      "date": "1916"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Medical and Military Response",
      "text": "During World War I, military and medical authorities began to address shell shock, with varying approaches ranging from rest and recuperation to disciplinary action.",
      "date": "1917"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Post-War Understanding and Treatment",
      "text": "After World War I, the understanding of shell shock evolved, leading to more humane treatment and the eventual development of the concept of post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD).",
      "date": "1918"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Transition to PTSD",
      "text": "In the latter half of the 20th century, the term 'shell shock' was largely replaced by 'post-traumatic stress disorder' (PTSD), reflecting a broader understanding of trauma-related conditions.",
      "date": "1980"
    }
  ]
}

