      "headline": "Air Florida Flight 90 Crash",
      "text": "On January 13, 1982, Air Florida Flight 90, a Boeing 737-200 registered as N62AF, crashed into the 14th Street Bridge over the Potomac River shortly after takeoff from Washington National Airport. The crash occurred during a snowstorm, and the aircraft struck the bridge before plunging into the icy river.",
      "date": "1982-01-13"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rescue Efforts",
      "text": "Following the crash, rescue efforts were hampered by the severe weather conditions. The U.S. Park Police helicopter and civilian bystanders played crucial roles in rescuing survivors from the icy waters of the Potomac River.",
      "date": "1982-01-13"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Investigation and Findings",
      "text": "The National Transportation Safety Board (NTSB) conducted an investigation into the crash. The final report, released later in 1982, cited pilot error and improper de-icing procedures as primary causes of the accident.",
      "date": "1982"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Impact and Changes",
      "text": "The crash of Air Florida Flight 90 led to significant changes in airline safety regulations, particularly concerning de-icing procedures and pilot training for winter weather conditions.",
      "date": "1982"
    }
  ]
}

