      "headline": "Formation of the Kingdom of Burgundy",
      "text": "The Kingdom of Burgundy was established in the early 5th century by the Burgundians, a Germanic tribe. They settled in the region after being displaced by the Huns.",
      "date": "-0400"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Creation of the Duchy of Burgundy",
      "text": "In 1032, the Duchy of Burgundy was created as a fief of the Kingdom of France. It became a significant political entity in medieval Europe.",
      "date": "1032"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Philip the Bold becomes Duke of Burgundy",
      "text": "In 1363, Philip the Bold, the youngest son of King John II of France, was granted the Duchy of Burgundy, marking the beginning of the Valois dynasty's rule.",
      "date": "1363"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Marriage of Philip the Bold and Margaret III",
      "text": "In 1369, Philip the Bold married Margaret III, Countess of Flanders, which significantly expanded the territories under Burgundian control.",
      "date": "1369"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reign of Charles the Bold",
      "text": "Charles the Bold ruled as Duke of Burgundy from 1467 until his death in 1477. His ambitions to expand his territories led to conflicts with France and the Holy Roman Empire.",
      "date": "1467"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Charles the Bold",
      "text": "Charles the Bold died in the Battle of Nancy on January 5, 1477, marking the end of the independent Duchy of Burgundy.",
      "date": "1477-01-05"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Annexation by France",
      "text": "Following the death of Charles the Bold, the Duchy of Burgundy was annexed by France in 1477, significantly altering the political landscape of the region.",
      "date": "1477"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Integration into the French Kingdom",
      "text": "By the early 16th century, Burgundy was fully integrated into the Kingdom of France, losing its status as an independent political entity.",
      "date": "1500"
    }
  ]
}

