      "headline": "Call for the Fourth Crusade",
      "text": "In 1198, Pope Innocent III called for a new crusade with the intent to recapture Jerusalem from Muslim control.",
      "date": "1198"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Crusaders Gather in Venice",
      "text": "In 1202, Crusaders gathered in Venice to set sail for the Holy Land. However, they lacked sufficient funds to pay for the Venetian fleet.",
      "date": "1202"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Siege of Zara",
      "text": "In November 1202, the Crusaders, unable to pay their debts to the Venetians, agreed to assist in the siege of Zara, a Christian city, as payment.",
      "date": "1202-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Diversion to Constantinople",
      "text": "In 1203, the Crusaders were diverted to Constantinople to restore the deposed Byzantine Emperor Isaac II Angelos, as part of a deal with his son, Alexios IV.",
      "date": "1203"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sack of Constantinople",
      "text": "On April 12, 1204, the Crusaders breached the walls of Constantinople and sacked the city, marking a significant deviation from their original mission.",
      "date": "1204-04-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Partition of the Byzantine Empire",
      "text": "Following the sack of Constantinople, the Crusaders and Venetians divided the Byzantine Empire among themselves, leading to the establishment of the Latin Empire and the period known as Frankokratia.",
      "date": "1204"
    }
  ]
}

