      "headline": "Introduction of Buddhism to Tibet",
      "text": "Buddhism was first introduced to Tibet during the reign of King Songtsen Gampo in the 7th century. He is credited with establishing Buddhism as a major religion in Tibet.",
      "date": "0600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Foundation of Samye Monastery",
      "text": "In 779, the Samye Monastery, the first Buddhist monastery in Tibet, was founded under the patronage of King Trisong Detsen. It became a key center for the dissemination of Buddhism in Tibet.",
      "date": "0779"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reign of King Trisong Detsen",
      "text": "King Trisong Detsen, who reigned from 755 to 797, played a crucial role in establishing Buddhism in Tibet by inviting Indian Buddhist scholars to his court.",
      "date": "0755"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Great Debate at Samye",
      "text": "The Great Debate at Samye, which took place around 792-794, was a significant event where Indian and Chinese Buddhist scholars debated the nature of enlightenment and the path to achieve it.",
      "date": "0792"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Era of Fragmentation",
      "text": "Following the collapse of the Tibetan Empire in the 9th century, Tibet entered an Era of Fragmentation, during which Buddhism's influence waned until its revival in the 10th century.",
      "date": "0842"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Revival of Tibetan Buddhism",
      "text": "The revival of Tibetan Buddhism began in the late 10th century, marked by the establishment of new monasteries and the translation of Buddhist texts.",
      "date": "0970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of the Gelug School",
      "text": "The Gelug school, one of the major schools of Tibetan Buddhism, was founded by Je Tsongkhapa in the late 14th century. It became the dominant school in Tibet.",
      "date": "1409"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of the Dalai Lama Lineage",
      "text": "The Dalai Lama lineage was established in the 15th century, with the first Dalai Lama, Gendun Drup, recognized in 1391.",
      "date": "1391"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Fifth Dalai Lama's Reign",
      "text": "The Fifth Dalai Lama, Ngawang Lobsang Gyatso, ruled from 1642 to 1682 and unified Tibet under his leadership, establishing the Ganden Phodrang government.",
      "date": "1642"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Tibetan Buddhism in Exile",
      "text": "Following the 1959 Tibetan uprising, the 14th Dalai Lama fled to India, leading to the establishment of Tibetan Buddhism in exile.",
      "date": "1959"
    }
  ]
}

