      "headline": "Construction of Hawa Mahal",
      "text": "The Hawa Mahal was constructed in 1799 by Maharaja Sawai Pratap Singh. It was designed by Lal Chand Ustad in the form of the crown of Krishna, the Hindu god.",
      "date": "1799"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Purpose of Hawa Mahal",
      "text": "The Hawa Mahal was built to allow the royal ladies to observe everyday life and festivals celebrated in the street below without being seen, as they had to obey the strict rules of 'purdah', which forbade them from appearing in public without face coverings.",
      "date": "1799"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Architectural Significance",
      "text": "The palace is a five-story pyramidal structure made up of 953 small windows called 'jharokhas' decorated with intricate latticework. The original intention of the lattice was to allow royal ladies to observe everyday life and festivals celebrated in the street below without being seen.",
      "date": "1799"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Renovation of Hawa Mahal",
      "text": "In 2006, renovation work was undertaken to preserve the historical structure. The project was funded by the Unit Trust of India.",
      "date": "2006"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Hawa Mahal as a Tourist Attraction",
      "text": "Today, Hawa Mahal is a popular tourist attraction in Jaipur and is recognized as an iconic symbol of the city. It attracts thousands of visitors each year who come to admire its unique architecture and historical significance.",
      "date": "2023"
    }
  ]
}

