      "text": "Herod Antipas was born around 20 BCE as a son of Herod the Great and Malthace, a Samaritan woman.",
      "date": "-0020"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Herod Antipas Becomes Tetrarch",
      "text": "In 4 BCE, following the death of his father Herod the Great, Herod Antipas was appointed tetrarch of Galilee and Perea by the Roman Emperor Augustus.",
      "date": "-0004"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Marriage to Herodias",
      "text": "Herod Antipas divorced his first wife, the daughter of King Aretas IV of Nabatea, and married Herodias, his brother's former wife, around 27 CE. This marriage was controversial and criticized by John the Baptist.",
      "date": "0027"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Execution of John the Baptist",
      "text": "Around 29 CE, Herod Antipas ordered the execution of John the Baptist, who had been imprisoned for criticizing Herod's marriage to Herodias.",
      "date": "0029"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Involvement in the Trial of Jesus",
      "text": "In 30 or 33 CE, Herod Antipas played a role in the trial of Jesus of Nazareth. According to the New Testament, Jesus was sent to Herod by Pontius Pilate, but Herod returned him to Pilate without making a judgment.",
      "date": "0030"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Exile to Gaul",
      "text": "In 39 CE, Herod Antipas was accused of conspiracy against the Roman Emperor Caligula and was exiled to Gaul, where he spent the rest of his life.",
      "date": "0039"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Herod Antipas",
      "text": "Herod Antipas died in exile in Gaul around 39 CE.",
      "date": "0039"
    }
  ]
}

