      "headline": "Enrolled at the Académie Royale des Beaux-Arts",
      "text": "In 1916, Magritte enrolled at the Académie Royale des Beaux-Arts in Brussels, where he studied until 1918.",
      "date": "1916"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Marriage to Georgette Berger",
      "text": "René Magritte married Georgette Berger in 1922, who would become a frequent model in his works.",
      "date": "1922"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Surrealist Painting",
      "text": "In 1926, Magritte painted 'The Lost Jockey,' considered his first surrealist work.",
      "date": "1926"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Solo Exhibition",
      "text": "Magritte held his first solo exhibition in Brussels in 1927, showcasing his surrealist works.",
      "date": "1927"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Moved to Paris",
      "text": "In 1927, Magritte moved to Paris, where he became involved with the Surrealist group led by André Breton.",
      "date": "1927"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Return to Brussels",
      "text": "Magritte returned to Brussels in 1930 after spending three years in Paris.",
      "date": "1930"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Treachery of Images",
      "text": "In 1929, Magritte painted 'The Treachery of Images,' featuring the famous phrase 'Ceci n'est pas une pipe.'",
      "date": "1929"
    },
    {
      "headline": "World War II Period",
      "text": "During World War II, Magritte remained in Brussels and adopted a brighter, more colorful style known as his 'Renoir Period.'",
      "date": "1940"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Human Condition",
      "text": "In 1933, Magritte painted 'The Human Condition,' exploring themes of perception and reality.",
      "date": "1933"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of René Magritte",
      "text": "René Magritte died on August 15, 1967, in Brussels, Belgium, leaving behind a significant legacy in the art world.",
      "date": "1967-08-15"
    }
  ]
}

