      "headline": "Beginning of the Neogene Period",
      "text": "The Neogene Period began approximately 23.03 million years ago, marking the end of the Paleogene Period. It is the second period of the Cenozoic Era.",
      "date": "-23030000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Miocene Epoch",
      "text": "The Miocene Epoch is the first epoch of the Neogene Period, spanning from about 23.03 million years ago to 5.333 million years ago. It was a time of significant geological and climatic changes.",
      "date": "-23030000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Pliocene Epoch",
      "text": "The Pliocene Epoch is the second epoch of the Neogene Period, lasting from about 5.333 million years ago to 2.58 million years ago. It was characterized by further climatic cooling and the formation of modern ecosystems.",
      "date": "-5333000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of the Neogene Period",
      "text": "The Neogene Period ended approximately 2.58 million years ago, transitioning into the Quaternary Period. This marked significant climatic shifts and the onset of the ice ages.",
      "date": "-2580000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Coining of the Term 'Neogene'",
      "text": "The term 'Neogene' was coined in 1853 by Austrian palaeontologist Moritz Hörnes. It was introduced to describe the geological period following the Paleogene.",
      "date": "1853"
    }
  ]
}

