      "headline": "Start of the Great Smog",
      "text": "The Great Smog of London began on Friday, 5 December 1952. A period of unusually cold weather, combined with an anticyclone and windless conditions, led to the accumulation of airborne pollutants, mostly from coal use, forming a thick layer of smog over the city.",
      "date": "1952-12-05"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peak of the Great Smog",
      "text": "By the weekend of 6-7 December 1952, the smog had become so dense that visibility was reduced to a few meters, and transportation was severely disrupted. The smog was particularly thick in central London.",
      "date": "1952-12-06"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of the Great Smog",
      "text": "The Great Smog of London dispersed on Tuesday, 9 December 1952, when the weather changed, and the wind picked up, clearing the smog.",
      "date": "1952-12-09"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Impact and Aftermath",
      "text": "The Great Smog of London caused significant health impacts, leading to an estimated 4,000 to 12,000 deaths due to respiratory issues and other illnesses exacerbated by the smog. It prompted changes in environmental policies, including the Clean Air Act of 1956.",
      "date": "1952-12"
    }
  ]
}

