      "headline": "Earliest Evidence of Trepanning",
      "text": "The earliest evidence of trepanning dates back to the Neolithic period, around 7000 BCE. Skulls with trepanation holes have been found in various parts of the world, including Europe, Africa, and South America.",
      "date": "-7000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Trepanning in Ancient Egypt",
      "text": "Trepanning was practiced in ancient Egypt, as evidenced by skulls from the pre-dynastic period (around 3000 BCE) that show signs of surgical intervention.",
      "date": "-3000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Trepanning in Ancient Greece",
      "text": "Hippocrates, the 'Father of Medicine', described trepanning in his medical texts around 400 BCE. He detailed the procedure and its use for treating head injuries.",
      "date": "-0400"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Trepanning in the Middle Ages",
      "text": "During the Middle Ages, trepanning was used in Europe to treat head injuries and mental disorders. It was believed to release evil spirits causing illness.",
      "date": "1000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Renaissance Advancements in Trepanning",
      "text": "In the Renaissance period, around the 16th century, advancements in surgical techniques and instruments improved the practice of trepanning. Surgeons like Ambroise Paré contributed to its development.",
      "date": "1500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modern Medical Use of Trepanning",
      "text": "In the 19th and 20th centuries, trepanning evolved into modern neurosurgical procedures. It is now used to relieve pressure from brain injuries and treat conditions like subdural hematomas.",
      "date": "1900"
    }
  ]
}

