      "headline": "First Recording of Human EEG",
      "text": "In 1924, German physiologist Hans Berger recorded the first human electroencephalogram (EEG). He was able to demonstrate the electrical activity of the human brain using a galvanometer.",
      "date": "1924"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of EEG Findings",
      "text": "In 1929, Hans Berger published his findings on the human EEG, which included the discovery of the alpha wave (8–12 Hz), also known as the Berger wave.",
      "date": "1929"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of the International 10–20 System",
      "text": "In 1958, the International 10–20 system was developed to standardize the placement of EEG electrodes on the scalp, facilitating consistent and comparable EEG recordings.",
      "date": "1958"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Computerized EEG Analysis",
      "text": "In the 1970s, the introduction of computerized EEG analysis allowed for more detailed and quantitative analysis of EEG data, enhancing the clinical and research applications of EEG.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Advancements in EEG Technology",
      "text": "In the late 20th and early 21st centuries, advancements in EEG technology, including digital EEG and portable EEG devices, expanded the use of EEG in clinical and research settings.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

