      "headline": "Emergence of Australopithecus",
      "text": "Australopithecus first appeared in Africa during the Pliocene epoch, approximately 4.2 million years ago. This genus is considered one of the earliest hominins and played a crucial role in human evolution.",
      "date": "-4200000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Australopithecus afarensis",
      "text": "Australopithecus afarensis lived between about 3.9 and 2.9 million years ago. This species is one of the best-known due to the discovery of the famous 'Lucy' fossil in 1974 in Ethiopia.",
      "date": "-3900000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Australopithecus africanus",
      "text": "Australopithecus africanus existed from about 3 to 2 million years ago. It was first discovered in South Africa and is known for its more human-like cranial features compared to earlier species.",
      "date": "-3000000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Australopithecus garhi",
      "text": "Australopithecus garhi lived around 2.5 million years ago. This species is notable for its association with some of the earliest known stone tools.",
      "date": "-2500000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Australopithecus sediba",
      "text": "Australopithecus sediba lived approximately 1.98 million years ago. Discovered in South Africa, it shows a mix of Australopithecus and Homo features, suggesting it may be a transitional species.",
      "date": "-1980000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Australopithecus anamensis",
      "text": "Australopithecus anamensis lived from about 4.2 to 3.9 million years ago. It is considered one of the earliest species of Australopithecus, with fossils found in Kenya and Ethiopia.",
      "date": "-4200000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Australopithecus bahrelghazali",
      "text": "Australopithecus bahrelghazali lived around 3.5 million years ago. It is known from fossils found in Chad, representing the first Australopithecus species discovered in Central Africa.",
      "date": "-3500000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Australopithecus deyiremeda",
      "text": "Australopithecus deyiremeda lived around 3.5 to 3.3 million years ago. Discovered in Ethiopia, this species is characterized by its distinct dental and jaw features.",
      "date": "-3500000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Extinction of Australopithecus",
      "text": "The genus Australopithecus became extinct around 1.9 million years ago, with its various species giving rise to or being replaced by other hominin genera such as Homo, Paranthropus, and Kenyanthropus.",
      "date": "-1900000"
    }
  ]
}

