      "text": "In the 8th century, Lalitaditya Muktapida of the Karkota dynasty ruled over Kashmir, including Srinagar, and expanded the region's influence.",
      "date": "0700"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sultanate of Kashmir",
      "text": "In the 14th century, the Shah Mir dynasty established the Sultanate of Kashmir, with Srinagar as its capital.",
      "date": "1339"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Mughal Conquest",
      "text": "In 1586, the Mughal Empire, under Emperor Akbar, annexed Kashmir, including Srinagar, integrating it into the Mughal Empire.",
      "date": "1586"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sikh Rule",
      "text": "In 1819, the Sikh Empire, led by Maharaja Ranjit Singh, conquered Srinagar, ending centuries of Muslim rule.",
      "date": "1819"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Dogra Rule",
      "text": "In 1846, after the First Anglo-Sikh War, the Treaty of Amritsar was signed, and the British sold Kashmir, including Srinagar, to Gulab Singh of the Dogra dynasty.",
      "date": "1846"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Partition of India",
      "text": "In 1947, following the partition of India, Srinagar became a focal point of conflict between India and Pakistan over the Kashmir region.",
      "date": "1947"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Summer Capital of Jammu and Kashmir",
      "text": "Srinagar was designated as the summer capital of the Indian-administered state of Jammu and Kashmir.",
      "date": "1952"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Jammu and Kashmir Reorganization Act",
      "text": "On October 31, 2019, the Jammu and Kashmir Reorganization Act came into effect, reconstituting the state into two union territories, with Srinagar as the summer capital of Jammu and Kashmir.",
      "date": "2019-10-31"
    }
  ]
}

