      "headline": "First Description of Pellagra",
      "text": "Pellagra was first described in 1735 by Spanish physician Gaspar Casal in Spain. He observed the disease in poor peasants and linked it to a diet primarily consisting of corn.",
      "date": "1735"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Pellagra Identified in Italy",
      "text": "In the 18th century, pellagra was identified in Italy, where it became known as 'mal de la rosa' due to the red rash associated with the disease.",
      "date": "1700s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Pellagra Epidemic in the United States",
      "text": "In the early 1900s, pellagra became a significant public health issue in the southern United States, affecting thousands of people due to poor diets lacking in niacin.",
      "date": "1900s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Discovery of Niacin Deficiency",
      "text": "In 1937, Conrad Elvehjem and his colleagues discovered that pellagra was caused by a deficiency of niacin (vitamin B3) and that the disease could be cured with niacin supplementation.",
      "date": "1937"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reduction of Pellagra Cases",
      "text": "Following the discovery of niacin's role in preventing pellagra, fortification of foods with niacin and improved dietary practices led to a significant reduction in pellagra cases.",
      "date": "1940s"
    }
  ]
}

