      "headline": "Sadi Carnot's Publication",
      "text": "In 1824, Sadi Carnot published 'Reflections on the Motive Power of Fire', which is considered the founding work of thermodynamics. He introduced the concept of the Carnot cycle and laid the groundwork for the second law of thermodynamics.",
      "date": "1824"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rudolf Clausius and the Second Law",
      "text": "In 1850, Rudolf Clausius formulated the second law of thermodynamics, introducing the concept of entropy and stating that heat cannot spontaneously flow from a colder body to a hotter body.",
      "date": "1850"
    },
    {
      "headline": "William Thomson (Lord Kelvin) and Absolute Temperature",
      "text": "In 1851, William Thomson, later known as Lord Kelvin, introduced the concept of absolute temperature, which led to the development of the Kelvin scale.",
