      "text": "The fasces originated in the Etruscan civilization, where it symbolized power and authority. The Etruscans were an ancient civilization in Italy, predating the Roman Empire.",
      "date": "-0800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adoption by Ancient Rome",
      "text": "The fasces were adopted by ancient Rome, where they symbolized a Roman king's power to punish his subjects. This symbol was later used to represent a magistrate's power and jurisdiction.",
      "date": "-0500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Use in Roman Republic",
      "text": "During the Roman Republic, fasces were carried by lictors who accompanied magistrates, symbolizing their authority and the power to enforce laws.",
      "date": "-0509"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Symbolism of the Axe",
      "text": "The axe within the fasces has its own separate and older origin, initially associated with the labrys, a double-bitted axe from Crete, one of the oldest symbols of Greek civilization.",
      "date": "-2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Fasces in the Roman Empire",
      "text": "In the Roman Empire, the fasces continued to be a symbol of power and authority, often depicted in art and carried in processions.",
      "date": "-0027"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Revival in Modern Times",
      "text": "The fasces were revived as a symbol of power and authority in various modern contexts, including its use by the Italian Fascist Party in the 20th century.",
