      "headline": "Pacific Railway Act Signed",
      "text": "On July 1, 1862, President Abraham Lincoln signed the Pacific Railway Act, which provided federal support for the construction of the first transcontinental railroad.",
      "date": "1862-07-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Construction Begins",
      "text": "In 1863, construction of the transcontinental railroad began. The Central Pacific Railroad started building eastward from Sacramento, California.",
      "date": "1863"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Union Pacific Railroad Construction Begins",
      "text": "In December 1863, the Union Pacific Railroad began construction westward from Omaha, Nebraska.",
      "date": "1863-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Western Pacific Railroad Construction",
      "text": "The Western Pacific Railroad Company built 132 miles of track from the western terminus at Alameda/Oakland to Sacramento, California, starting in 1865.",
      "date": "1865"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Central Pacific Reaches Summit Tunnel",
      "text": "In 1867, the Central Pacific Railroad reached the Summit Tunnel through the Sierra Nevada mountains, a significant engineering challenge.",
      "date": "1867"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Completion of the First Transcontinental Railroad",
      "text": "On May 10, 1869, the First Transcontinental Railroad was completed at Promontory Summit, Utah Territory, with the ceremonial driving of the golden spike.",
      "date": "1869-05-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Railroad Opens for Through Traffic",
      "text": "On May 15, 1869, the transcontinental railroad opened for through traffic, connecting the eastern U.S. rail network with the Pacific coast.",
      "date": "1869-05-15"
    }
  ]
}

