      "headline": "Formation of the Indian National Army",
      "text": "The Indian National Army (INA) was founded by Mohan Singh in September 1942 in Southeast Asia during World War II. It was formed with the aim of securing Indian independence from British rule with the help of the Japanese Empire.",
      "date": "1942-09"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Leadership of Subhas Chandra Bose",
      "text": "In 1943, Subhas Chandra Bose took over the leadership of the Indian National Army. Under his command, the INA was reorganized and expanded, and it became a significant force in the Indian independence movement.",
      "date": "1943"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Proclamation of the Provisional Government of Free India",
      "text": "On October 21, 1943, Subhas Chandra Bose proclaimed the establishment of the Provisional Government of Free India, or Azad Hind, in Singapore. This government was recognized by several Axis powers.",
      "date": "1943-10-21"
    },
    {
      "headline": "INA's Role in the Battle of Imphal",
      "text": "In 1944, the Indian National Army participated in the Battle of Imphal, which was part of the Japanese offensive against British India. Despite initial successes, the campaign ultimately failed, and the INA suffered heavy casualties.",
      "date": "1944"
    },
    {
      "headline": "INA Trials",
      "text": "After World War II, in 1945, several officers of the Indian National Army were put on trial by the British at the Red Fort in Delhi. These trials sparked widespread nationalist sentiments and protests across India.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Disbandment of the Indian National Army",
      "text": "Following the end of World War II and the trials, the Indian National Army was disbanded in 1945. The INA's efforts, however, had a lasting impact on the Indian independence movement.",
      "date": "1945"
    }
  ]
}

